WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.832
Rolling calculations are useful when we want to understand short-term trends.

00:00:04.832 --> 00:00:08.722
A rolling window looks at a group of nearby observations.

00:00:08.722 --> 00:00:16.207
In this example, the window size is three, so each rolling mean uses three consecutive sales values.

00:00:16.207 --> 00:00:22.178
This creates a smoother series that can make patterns easier to see than the original daily values.

00:00:22.628 --> 00:00:26.640
You may notice missing values at the beginning of the rolling average.

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:33.895
That happens because a three-row window needs three observations before a complete average can be calculated.

00:00:33.895 --> 00:00:39.890
In some situations, you can change that behavior using the min periods argument,

00:00:39.890 --> 00:00:44.375
but first make sure you understand what the default result means.

00:00:44.375 --> 00:00:50.040
Run the code and compare the original sales column with the rolling three-day average.

00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:53.280
Then change the window from three to five.

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:59.756
A larger window usually produces a smoother trend, but it can also hide short-term changes.
